# Read boundary for `openwiki` (mise run openwiki-init / openwiki-update). Paths listed here are
# never read, scanned, or reproduced in the generated wiki.
#
# This is not a taste filter. Everything below is either a byte-for-byte duplicate of source that
# openwiki already reads, or generated output whose staleness would be laundered into the wiki as
# fresh evidence.

# Task worktrees are FULL CHECKOUTS of this same repository, each on a different branch. Left
# readable, openwiki would ground claims in dozens of divergent copies of every file it documents.
.claude/worktrees/

# Generated agent documentation (output of /map-codebase). Grounding a generated wiki on an older
# generated map turns "the map said so" into evidence, which is exactly the drift openwiki's Claims
# exist to catch. The hand-written docs/guides/ and docs/harnessed-design.md stay readable.
docs/codebase/

# `tests/` is NOT ignored, deliberately. It is where this project's contracts are actually pinned —
# `mise.toml` cites `test_external_contracts_live.py` by name as the reason varlock is pinned, and
# `install.sh` precedence is asserted as ORDER in `test_install_script.py::TestPrecedence`. Excluded,
# the wiki has to infer those guarantees instead of citing them. See INSTRUCTIONS.md for the rule
# that keeps that from turning into 2 MB of enumerated test bodies.
#
# `tools/` is NOT ignored either, narrowed back to the generated fixture profiles it was meant to
# cover. Excluding the whole directory made the wiki describe `tools/run-tests.sh`,
# `tools/preflight.sh` and `tools/openwiki-drift.py` from `mise.toml` and the CI workflows alone,
# without being able to open any of them — which is how `testing/verification-ladder.md` came to
# overstate what the drift gate verifies (raised in review on #445).

